; Leg BK -- block check alone.  All x block 1, PA's other variable moved.
;
; This is the leg round 1 got right by accident, and it is the port's best
; result: 27.00 s, 37,534 wire bytes, rxlost=0 rxfull=0, zero resends,
; zero timeouts, byte-exact.  719us per wire byte against SS16v leg CA's
; 906 on the same wire-byte count -- a nearly controlled A/B, since the
; two differ by 2 bytes of traffic and by the block check.
;
; That prices chk3() at about 130us per wire byte, ~15% of the receive
; cost.  The estimate it replaces was ~60us, from SS16t's instruction-fetch
; model; THE MODEL WAS LOW BY 2x.  Which also means the estimate for the
; proposed chk3() long -> unsigned int upstream edit (~2.3%) was built on
; the wrong number and is nearer 5%.
;
; RE-RUN THIS ONE MORE THAN ONCE.  It is a single measurement carrying the
; whole conclusion, and round 1 demonstrated this bench has run-to-run
; variance big enough to invent a finding: legs PA and PC were the same
; experiment twice over and returned rxpeak 2,006 and 4,095.
;
; Block check 1 is a 6-bit checksum -- weaker than CRC-16.  The bench cable
; has shown zero errors at 38400 (SS16t, SS16v, and PA/BK here), which is
; what makes it measurable.  It is NOT by itself an argument for shipping
; it as the default, and the far end's line quality is not something this
; port can assume.

set line /dev/tty.usbserial-ABBFKXM1
set speed 38400
set receive timeout 20
set parity none
set prefixing all
set block 1
log packets s16aeBK.pkt
send rcvbk.dat
statistics
